# Phase 12 WP 4.7 — Content-Security-Policy snippet (nonce-based).
#
# IMPORT pattern: in any Caddyfile site block:
#   import csp-report-only   # Step 1 — collect violations
#   import csp-enforce       # Step 2 — flip to enforcing
#
# Two-step rollout (per WP 4.7 spec):
#   Step 1 (week 1): `csp-report-only` snippet — Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only
#                    header collects violations to /csp-report endpoint without
#                    blocking. Operator reviews violations + fixes offending code.
#   Step 2 (week 2): `csp-enforce` snippet — flip to enforcing Content-Security-Policy
#                    header once 0 violations for 3 consecutive days.
#
# Nonce injection: Caddy's `{http.request.uuid}` placeholder provides a
# per-request unique value. Vite's `vite-plugin-csp-nonce` (if added) or
# server-side HTML template engine substitutes `{{nonce}}` placeholders
# in <script nonce="..."> and <style nonce="...">.
#
# Without nonce-substitution at HTML render: inline scripts blocked.
# That's the GOAL — forces all inline code to be either:
#   - Externalized to a separate .js file (preferred for CSP-strict)
#   - Marked with nonce="{{nonce}}" placeholder Caddy substitutes
#
# CSP policy rationale per directive:
#   default-src 'self'   → block all cross-origin loads by default
#   script-src           → only same-origin + nonce-tagged inline allowed
#                          (NO 'unsafe-inline', NO 'unsafe-eval')
#   style-src            → 'unsafe-inline' is a transition compromise for
#                          tailwind utilities + Radix; revisit when migrated
#                          to all-external CSS (WP 4.7b TBD)
#   img-src              → 'self' + data: (avatars/icons) + https: (Sentry)
#   connect-src          → 'self' + wss: (WebSocket) + *.${PUBLIC_TLD} subdomains
#   frame-ancestors      → none (modern X-Frame-Options=DENY equivalent)
#   base-uri             → 'self' (prevents <base> tag injection)
#   form-action          → 'self' (prevents form redirect to attacker domain)
#   report-uri           → /csp-report → Sentry CSP-report endpoint

# Step 1: Report-Only mode (use during initial rollout)
(csp-report-only) {
  header Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'nonce-{http.request.uuid}'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https:; connect-src 'self' wss: https://*.{$PUBLIC_TLD} https://*.{$INTERNAL_TLD}; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; report-uri /csp-report"
}

# Step 2: Enforcing mode (flip after 7-day soak with 0 violations)
(csp-enforce) {
  header Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'nonce-{http.request.uuid}'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https:; connect-src 'self' wss: https://*.{$PUBLIC_TLD} https://*.{$INTERNAL_TLD}; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; report-uri /csp-report"
}

# Companion: CSP-report endpoint forwards browser violation reports to
# Sentry. Mount as a separate site block in the Caddyfile:
#
#   /csp-report {
#     reverse_proxy https://sentry.io/api/0/csp-report/{SENTRY_PROJECT_ID}/?sentry_key={SENTRY_PUBLIC_KEY}
#   }
#
# Operator wires SENTRY_PROJECT_ID + SENTRY_PUBLIC_KEY in Coolify env.
